The current board has no immediate winning move for o (Player o) in any legal column (0, 3, 4, 5 or 6). Column 4 is immediately losing: placing in column 4 opens the square (2,4), allowing x to complete the diagonal x-x-x-(2,4) on the following turn. Consequently both players must avoid column 4 for the remainder of the game unless a forced win already exists on that move.

Column 0 is the unique move that creates a forced threat on the subsequent turn. After o drops at (3,0):
- any reply by x inside column 0 itself lets o finish at (1,0) and win at once with the horizontal four already present in row 1;
- therefore x must play elsewhere (a “free” placement that creates no counter-threat of its own);
- o then returns to column 0 at (2,0). The square (1,0) is now the lowest empty cell and completes the same horizontal four, producing an immediate threat;
- x is compelled to block at (1,0).

The net result is that o has spent two moves, x has been forced to spend one move answering the threat, and o retains the initiative. From the resulting position o can next begin the analogous forcing sequence on the right side (column 5) that ultimately compels x to open the poisoned column 4; once that occurs the follow-up (2,4)-(1,4)-(0,4) wins for o on the long diagonal already occupied by three o discs. No other first-move column generates a comparable forced threat while simultaneously preserving the later winning plan.

Final Answer: 0

